//! Files.
//!
//! A file capability comes from radix-directory(2)'s `open`. Reading needs
//! the read right and writing the write right. Reads and writes go
//! through the page cache: a read copies from the cache's pages (filling
//! them from the disk first), a write copies into them, and the disk sees
//! the change when its filesystem syncs (every five seconds, at `sync`,
//! and at unmount). The ring's read, write and sync operations
//! (radix-ring(2)) do the same without a system call each.
//!
//! Since Phase 8 a file capability is also what POSIX calls an open file
//! description: it has an offset, which reads and writes at CURRENT use
//! and move, and status flags. Copies of the capability (`derive`, and
//! `fork`'s copies) share them; each `open` makes a new one. Pipes
//! (radix-process(2)'s `pipe`) and terminals (radix-terminal(2)) are
//! files too, which have no offset: their reads and writes take any
//! offset, and their reads return what has come, waiting while nothing
//! has unless the description is non_blocking.
//!
//! Since Phase 9 sockets are files of that kind too: the ends of a socket
//! pair (radix-process(2)'s `socket_pair`), and sockets radix-process(2)'s
//! `socket` makes, which radix-directory(2) names and connects, and which
//! `listen` and `accept` take connections on. They have methods of their
//! own: `send` and `receive`, and with files, `send_files` and
//! `receive_files`; `shutdown`; `socket_name` and `socket_state`. Each
//! end of a connection reads what the other writes, as through two pipes,
//! one each way.
//!
//! Since Phase 12 network sockets are too (radix-network(2)'s `socket`,
//! ADR 0183): TCP's stream sockets and UDP's datagram sockets, which
//! `bind_address` binds and `connect_address` connects, through the
//! network capability whose slot they take, and which `send_to`,
//! `receive_from` and `socket_option` are for. `send`, `receive`,
//! `shutdown`, `listen`, `accept`, `socket_name` and `socket_state` work
//! on them as on the others; `send_files` and `receive_files` do not.

module file;

/// `control`'s request that makes a file share all of another's data,
/// Linux's FICLONE (ADR 0139): `argument` is the slot of the source, open
/// for reading, and the file is open for writing; `buffer` and `size` are
/// not used. The two are on one Btrfs filesystem (else cross_mount; a
/// filesystem that cannot clone raises not_supported), and the file's
/// data before it goes; a copy that takes no room until one of the two
/// changes.
const CLONE: u32 = 0x40049409;
/// `control`'s request that makes part of a file share part of another's
/// data, Linux's FICLONERANGE: `buffer` holds Linux's struct
/// file_clone_range, its `src_fd` the slot of the source, and `size` is
/// 32. The offsets and the length are whole blocks, but for a range that
/// ends at the source's end; a length of 0 goes to the source's end.
const CLONE_RANGE: u32 = 0x4020940d;

/// The offset of a write that appends: at the end of the file, wherever
/// that is when the write happens.
const APPEND: u64 = 0xffffffffffffffff;
/// The offset of a read or write at the description's offset, which then
/// moves past the bytes read or written (at the end, for a write when the
/// description appends).
const CURRENT: u64 = 0xfffffffffffffffe;

/// A time `set_times` leaves as it is.
const TIME_OMIT: u64 = 0xffffffffffffffff;
/// A time `set_times` sets to now, by the kernel's clock.
const TIME_NOW: u64 = 0xfffffffffffffffe;

/// Where `seek` counts from, as `lseek`'s `whence`.
enum Whence: u32 {
    /// From the start of the file.
    start = 0;
    /// From the description's offset.
    current = 1;
    /// From the end of the file.
    end = 2;
}

/// Whose a file lock is, which decides which other locks it meets and
/// when it goes. Locks are advisory: they stand in the way of other
/// locks, never of reads and writes.
enum LockOwner: u32 {
    /// The open file description's lock on the whole file, as BSD's
    /// `flock`: it meets only other such locks, and goes with the
    /// description's last capability.
    description = 0;
    /// The calling process's lock on a range of bytes, as POSIX's record
    /// locks (`fcntl`'s F_SETLK): it goes when the process ends, or
    /// deletes any capability of the file, and `fork`'s child has none of
    /// them.
    process = 1;
    /// The open file description's lock on a range of bytes, as Linux's
    /// open file description locks (F_OFD_SETLK): it meets process locks
    /// too, and goes with the description's last capability.
    description_range = 2;
}

/// What a file lock lets others do.
enum LockKind: u32 {
    /// No lock: `lock` lets go of the range, and `lock_holder` finds none
    /// in the way.
    none = 0;
    /// Others may hold shared locks on the range too, as readers do.
    shared = 1;
    /// Nobody else may lock the range, as a writer does.
    exclusive = 2;
}

/// An open file description's status flags.
flags DescriptionFlags: u32 {
    /// Writes at CURRENT go at the end of the file.
    append = 0;
    /// Reads and writes of a pipe or terminal that would wait fail with
    /// would_block instead.
    non_blocking = 1;
}

/// How `send` and `receive` go, for that call alone.
flags SocketFlags: u32 {
    /// Fail with would_block instead of waiting, as if the description
    /// were non_blocking: POSIX's MSG_DONTWAIT.
    dont_wait = 0;
    /// A send to an end no one reads fails with gone, without SIGPIPE:
    /// POSIX's MSG_NOSIGNAL.
    no_signal = 1;
    /// The slots `receive_files` puts files in are closed by exec:
    /// POSIX's MSG_CMSG_CLOEXEC.
    close_on_exec = 2;
    /// What a receive gets stays, for the next receive to get again:
    /// POSIX's MSG_PEEK. A stream socket's bytes may be fewer than wait;
    /// a datagram socket's datagram stays whole. Files riding on the
    /// bytes (`receive_files`) come as copies, and stay too, as on Linux.
    /// (Since Phase 12, ADR 0341.)
    peek = 3;
    /// A datagram or ping socket's `receive_from` and `receive_message`
    /// return the datagram's whole size, though only what `buffer` has
    /// room for is written and the rest is lost: Linux's MSG_TRUNC, which
    /// recvmsg(2) uses to say that a datagram was cut short. A stream
    /// socket's receive changes nothing. (Since Phase 16, ADR 0496.)
    whole_size = 4;
}

/// A network socket's option, which `socket_option` reads and sets, as
/// POSIX's `getsockopt` and `setsockopt` name them. A boolean is 0 or 1.
enum SocketOption: u32 {
    /// SO_REUSEADDR, a boolean. A port is free once the socket bound to
    /// it is closed, so for a stream socket it changes nothing, but it is
    /// kept; a datagram socket that sets it before it is bound shares its
    /// port as with reuse_port (since Phase 12c.3).
    reuse_address = 1;
    /// SO_KEEPALIVE, a boolean: a stream socket's connection is probed
    /// after two hours without traffic (keep_idle), and reset if the
    /// other end has gone: when 9 probes 75 seconds apart (keep_count,
    /// keep_interval) go unanswered.
    keep_alive = 2;
    /// TCP_NODELAY, a boolean: a stream socket sends small writes at once
    /// rather than gathering them (Nagle's algorithm).
    no_delay = 3;
    /// SO_RCVBUF: the bytes the socket holds as they come. Setting it is
    /// taken, and changes nothing.
    receive_buffer = 4;
    /// SO_SNDBUF: the bytes it holds as they go. Setting it is taken, and
    /// changes nothing.
    send_buffer = 5;
    /// SO_ERROR, read only: why the last connection the socket tried
    /// failed, as a status word (0 for none), which reading clears; for
    /// a datagram or ping socket, the error an ICMP or ICMPv6 error
    /// reported last (receive_errors; since Phase 12, ADR 0345).
    error = 6;
    /// IP_TTL: how many routers its packets may pass, 1 to 255.
    time_to_live = 7;
    /// SO_BROADCAST, a boolean: whether a datagram socket may send to a
    /// broadcast address, 255.255.255.255 or its network's; without it,
    /// such a send fails with broadcast.
    broadcast = 8;
    /// SO_TYPE, read only: the socket's SocketType (radix-network(2)).
    kind = 9;
    /// SO_RCVTIMEO: the most nanoseconds a receive or an accept waits, 0
    /// for no limit; one that waits longer fails with would_block.
    receive_timeout = 10;
    /// SO_SNDTIMEO: likewise for a send, and for a stream socket's
    /// connect, which then fails with in_progress.
    send_timeout = 11;
    /// TCP_KEEPIDLE: the seconds a stream socket's connection with
    /// keep_alive is quiet before its first probe, 1 to 32,767; 7,200 to
    /// begin with.
    keep_idle = 12;
    /// TCP_KEEPINTVL: the seconds between probes that go unanswered, 1 to
    /// 32,767; 75 to begin with.
    keep_interval = 13;
    /// TCP_KEEPCNT: the probes that may go unanswered before the
    /// connection is reset, 1 to 127; 9 to begin with.
    keep_count = 14;
    /// SO_BINDTODEVICE: the interface, by its number (radix-network(2)),
    /// that the socket's packets go out through and come in by, 0 for
    /// any. A datagram socket bound to an interface with no IPv4 address
    /// sends from 0.0.0.0, as a DHCP client does. Set before the socket
    /// connects or listens.
    bind_to_device = 15;
    /// IPV6_V6ONLY, a boolean, of an IPv6 socket's: whether it takes only
    /// IPv6, or IPv4 too through IPv4-mapped addresses (::ffff:10.0.2.2),
    /// as it does to begin with, as on Linux. Set before it is bound.
    /// (Since Phase 12c.)
    v6_only = 16;
    /// SO_DOMAIN, read only: the socket's AddressFamily
    /// (radix-network(2)). (Since Phase 12c.)
    family = 17;
    /// IP_MULTICAST_IF and IPV6_MULTICAST_IF: the interface, by its
    /// number, that a datagram socket's datagrams to a multicast group go
    /// out through, 0 for the one the socket is bound to or the routes
    /// say. (Since Phase 12c.3, ADR 0336.)
    multicast_interface = 18;
    /// IP_MULTICAST_TTL and IPV6_MULTICAST_HOPS: how many routers a
    /// datagram to a multicast group may pass, 0 to 255; 1 to begin
    /// with, so that it stays on its network. (Since Phase 12c.3.)
    multicast_hops = 19;
    /// IP_MULTICAST_LOOP and IPV6_MULTICAST_LOOP, a boolean, true to
    /// begin with: kept, though a datagram to a group goes to other
    /// machines alone, and this machine's own sockets that joined it do
    /// not get a copy. (Since Phase 12c.3.)
    multicast_loop = 20;
    /// SO_REUSEPORT, a boolean, of a datagram socket's, set before it is
    /// bound: it shares its port with the others that set this or
    /// reuse_address, as on Linux. A datagram to a group or a broadcast
    /// address reaches each of them; one to an address of this
    /// machine's, one of them. A stream socket's changes nothing. (Since
    /// Phase 12c.3.)
    reuse_port = 21;
    /// IP_PKTINFO and IPV6_RECVPKTINFO, a boolean: whether
    /// `receive_message` says that the program asked for where each
    /// datagram went and the interface it came by. (Since Phase 12c.3.)
    packet_info = 22;
    /// IP_RECVERR and IPV6_RECVERR, a boolean, of a datagram or ping
    /// socket's: the ICMP and ICMPv6 errors it hears about what it sent,
    /// a router's "time exceeded" and a machine's "port unreachable"
    /// among them, wait for `receive_error`, as on Linux, and each makes
    /// the next send or receive fail with its error, once. Without it,
    /// only a connected socket hears one, and only of the errors that
    /// say the other end cannot be reached at all (refused,
    /// protocol_unreachable, prohibited and the like). Turning it off
    /// throws away those that wait. (Since Phase 12, ADR 0345.)
    receive_errors = 23;
    /// IP_TOS and IPV6_TCLASS: the type of service a socket's packets
    /// would carry, 0 to 255. It is taken and read back, as on Linux, but
    /// every packet still goes with 0 (ADR 0345, ADR 0410): what is on the
    /// wire is the kernel's to choose.
    type_of_service = 24;
    /// SO_LINGER, of a stream socket's: 0, as it begins, for a close
    /// that returns at once and lets the connection end in the
    /// background, sending what waits and then FIN; 1 for a close that
    /// resets the connection instead, throwing away what waits and
    /// sending RST, as Linux's linger of 0 seconds does; 1 plus a number
    /// of seconds for a linger that long, kept and read back, though the
    /// close still returns at once and the connection ends as with 0.
    /// (Since Phase 16, ADR 0496.)
    linger = 25;
    /// TCP_QUICKACK, a boolean, of a stream socket's: its connection
    /// acknowledges what comes at once, rather than after up to 10
    /// milliseconds in case an answer can carry the acknowledgement. It
    /// stays set until it is turned off, unlike Linux's, which the
    /// connection may turn off again by itself. (Since Phase 16, ADR
    /// 0496.)
    quick_ack = 26;
    /// SO_OOBINLINE, a boolean: taken and read back. A stream socket's
    /// urgent data always comes inline, among its other bytes, as with
    /// it set on Linux: Radix has no out-of-band receive. (Since Phase
    /// 16, ADR 0496.)
    out_of_band_inline = 27;
}

/// The most files one `send_files` sends, as Linux's SCM_MAX_FD.
const SEND_FILES_MAX: u64 = 253;
/// The most files on their way through one direction of a connection,
/// sent and not yet received.
const FILES_IN_FLIGHT_MAX: u64 = 1024;
/// The most connections a listening socket keeps waiting: SOMAXCONN.
const BACKLOG_MAX: u64 = 4096;
/// The longest name a socket takes, in bytes: sockaddr_un's sun_path,
/// without its NUL.
const SOCKET_NAME_MAX: u64 = 107;

/// A file.
interface file = 0x000c version 11 {
    kind = file;
    /// Reads from byte `offset` into `buffer`; returns how many bytes it
    /// read, fewer than asked at the end of the file. Needs the read right.
    method read = 1 (offset: u64, buffer: out bytes) -> (count: u64)
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, missing_rights, invalid_argument, bad_address, no_memory,
                is_directory, io, corrupt];
    /// Writes `data` at byte `offset` (APPEND: at the end), growing the
    /// file if need be; returns how many bytes it wrote. Needs the write
    /// right.
    method write = 2 (offset: u64, data: in bytes) -> (count: u64)
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, missing_rights, invalid_argument, bad_address, no_memory,
                is_directory, read_only, no_space, quota_exceeded, too_big, io, corrupt];
    /// Writes what the file is into `buffer`, as radix-directory(2)'s
    /// `stat` does.
    method stat = 3 (buffer: out bytes) -> ()
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, invalid_argument, bad_address];
    /// Makes the file `size` bytes long: cut, or grown with zeros. Needs
    /// the write right.
    method set_size = 4 (size: u64) -> ()
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, missing_rights, invalid_argument, no_memory, is_directory,
                read_only, no_space, quota_exceeded, too_big, io, corrupt];
    /// Makes the file's changes durable, and returns once the disk has
    /// them; a filesystem may write more of its changes with them, as FAT,
    /// exFAT and ext4 write all. Needs no right: as POSIX's fsync, it works
    /// on a file opened only for reading.
    method sync = 5 () -> ()
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, io, corrupt];
    /// Moves the description's offset to `offset` bytes from where
    /// `whence` says, and returns it. Pipes and terminals fail with
    /// not_seekable.
    method seek = 6 (offset: i64, whence: Whence) -> (offset: u64)
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, invalid_argument, not_seekable, io, corrupt];
    /// Reads, and with `set` changes, the description's status flags, and
    /// returns them as they were.
    method status_flags = 7 (set: bool, flags: DescriptionFlags) -> (flags: DescriptionFlags)
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot];
    /// A device's own request, as Linux's `ioctl`: `request` with its
    /// `argument`, and `size` bytes at `buffer` it reads or writes.
    /// Terminals take the termios, window size, process group and
    /// controlling terminal requests radix-terminal(2) lists, block
    /// devices the size, sector and discard requests radix-block(2) lists,
    /// packet captures CAPTURE_FILTER and CAPTURE_COUNTS (radix-network(2),
    /// ADR 0346), and files `CLONE` and `CLONE_RANGE`; other files fail
    /// with not_terminal.
    method control = 8 (request: u32, argument: u64, buffer: addr, size: u64) -> (result: u64)
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, missing_rights, invalid_argument, bad_address,
                not_terminal, not_permitted, interrupted, io, read_only, not_supported, no_memory,
                cross_mount, is_directory, no_space, quota_exceeded];
    /// Takes, changes or lets go of (`kind` none) a lock of `owner`'s on
    /// the `length` bytes from `start` (0: all those past it, however the
    /// file grows); a description lock always covers the whole file. A
    /// lock its owner holds on part of the range already gives way to the
    /// new one, which joins those of the same kind it touches. Another
    /// owner's lock in the way fails it with would_block, or with `wait`
    /// is waited for, until a signal interrupts the wait; a process lock
    /// whose holder waits, in a chain, for one the caller's process holds
    /// fails it with deadlock instead. A shared range lock needs the read
    /// right and an exclusive one the write right.
    method lock = 9 (owner: LockOwner, kind: LockKind, start: u64, length: u64, wait: bool) -> ()
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, missing_rights, invalid_argument, no_memory,
                would_block, interrupted, deadlock];
    /// Finds a lock that would stand in the way of a `kind` lock of
    /// `owner`'s on the range `lock` takes, and returns its kind (none if
    /// there is none), its range, and the number of the process holding
    /// it (a description's: 0xffffffffffffffff).
    method lock_holder = 10 (owner: LockOwner, kind: LockKind, start: u64, length: u64)
        -> (kind: LockKind, start: u64, length: u64, process: u64)
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, invalid_argument];
    /// Sets when the file was last read and when its contents last
    /// changed, in nanoseconds since 1970, or now (TIME_NOW), or leaves
    /// either (TIME_OMIT). The filesystem keeps them as finely as it can
    /// (FAT: two seconds, and the day for reads); reading a file never
    /// changes its read time (ADR 0076). Devices and pipes keep no times
    /// and fail with not_permitted. Needs the write right, and to set a
    /// time to one given, the file's owner or the bypass right
    /// (not_permitted), as radix-directory(2)'s `set_times`.
    method set_times = 11 (accessed: u64, modified: u64) -> ()
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, missing_rights, not_permitted, read_only, io, corrupt];
    /// Sends `data` to a socket's other end, as `write` does but with
    /// `flags`; returns how many bytes went. Sending to an end that is
    /// gone, or shut for reading, raises SIGPIPE (unless no_signal) and
    /// fails with gone. A datagram socket sends `data` as one datagram to
    /// the address it is connected to. Needs the write right.
    method send = 12 (data: in bytes, flags: SocketFlags) -> (count: u64)
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, missing_rights, bad_address, not_socket, would_block,
                gone, interrupted, not_connected, reset, message_too_long, unreachable, broadcast,
                refused, host_unreachable, protocol_error, host_down, no_network,
                protocol_unreachable, prohibited, not_supported];
    /// Receives what a socket's other end sent into `buffer`, as `read`
    /// does but with `flags`; returns how many bytes came, 0 once the
    /// other end is gone or shut for writing and all it sent has been
    /// read. A datagram socket receives the next datagram, of which what
    /// `buffer` has no room for is lost. Needs the read right.
    method receive = 13 (buffer: out bytes, flags: SocketFlags) -> (count: u64)
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, missing_rights, bad_address, not_socket, would_block,
                interrupted, not_connected, reset, refused, unreachable, host_unreachable,
                protocol_error, host_down, no_network, protocol_unreachable, prohibited,
                message_too_long, not_supported];
    /// Shuts a socket's end for reading (what the other end sends then
    /// fails with gone), for writing (the other end then receives the
    /// end), or both, for every description of the end: POSIX's
    /// `shutdown`.
    method shutdown = 14 (read: bool, write: bool) -> ()
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, not_socket, not_connected];
    /// Has a socket radix-process(2)'s `socket` made, which has a name
    /// (radix-directory(2)'s `bind_socket`) and is not connected, take
    /// connections: at most `backlog` wait for `accept` at once (1 at
    /// least, BACKLOG_MAX at most; 0 means 1). A socket that listens
    /// already takes the new number. A socket with no name, or connected,
    /// fails with invalid_argument. A network stream socket must be bound
    /// (`bind_address`) through a network capability with the receive
    /// right for its port, else missing_rights; the kernel holds up to
    /// `backlog` of its connections on each interface, 128 at most, being
    /// made or waiting, and leaves a SYN that comes when it holds that
    /// many unanswered, for its client to send again (ADR 0494). Needs the
    /// write right.
    method listen = 15 (backlog: u32) -> ()
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, missing_rights, invalid_argument, no_memory,
                not_socket, busy, no_buffers, not_supported];
    /// Takes a connection that waits at a listening socket, waiting for
    /// one unless the description is non_blocking, and puts a
    /// description of its end in slot `to` with `flags`: a socket of its
    /// own, connected, with the listening socket's name. Slot `to` must
    /// be empty, else occupied; a connection that cannot go in it, as
    /// another thread filled it while the call waited, goes back to the
    /// front of those that wait, so none is lost. A socket that does not
    /// listen fails with invalid_argument. Needs the read right.
    method accept = 16 (to: slot, flags: PipeFlags) -> ()
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, missing_rights, occupied, invalid_argument,
                no_memory, not_socket, would_block, interrupted];
    /// Writes the name of a socket (radix-directory(2)'s `bind_socket`),
    /// or with `peer` of the socket at a connection's other end, into
    /// `buffer`, as the path the binding was asked for; returns its
    /// length, 0 for none, which is more than `buffer` holds if it was
    /// cut short. A connection that `accept` took has the listening
    /// socket's name. For a network socket it is an Address
    /// (radix-network(2)): where it is bound, all zero if it is not, or
    /// where it is connected.
    method socket_name = 17 (peer: bool, buffer: out bytes) -> (len: u64)
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, bad_address, not_socket, not_connected];
    /// What a socket is: whether it listens (SO_ACCEPTCONN), whether it
    /// is connected, and for a connected one the process at the other end,
    /// SO_PEERCRED: its number, the user it acted for and that user's
    /// group (radix-names(2)). That is the process that connected, or for
    /// the connecting end, the one that listened, as each was when the
    /// connection was made or the listening began; for a pair's ends, the
    /// process that made the pair; for a network socket, 0 for all three.
    /// A service such as authd tells who asks it so.
    method socket_state = 18 () -> (listening: bool, connected: bool, peer: u64, user: u32,
                                    group: u32)
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, not_socket];
    /// Sends `data` as `send` does, with the files whose slots `slots`
    /// holds (u32 each, at most SEND_FILES_MAX): copies of their
    /// capabilities, with their rights, which go with the first byte of
    /// `data` and come out with it at the other end. Each must be a file
    /// or directory capability with the copy right, and no socket: a
    /// socket among them fails with not_supported. More than
    /// FILES_IN_FLIGHT_MAX on their way fails with too_many. `data` must
    /// hold a byte at least. Needs the write right.
    method send_files = 19 (data: in bytes, slots: in bytes, flags: SocketFlags) -> (count: u64)
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, wrong_kind, missing_rights, invalid_argument,
                no_memory, bad_address, not_socket, not_connected, not_supported, too_many,
                would_block, gone, interrupted];
    /// Receives as `receive` does, with the files that came with the
    /// bytes received: a receive that starts at a byte files came with
    /// takes them, and one that does not ends before such a byte. Puts
    /// each in the lowest free slot from radix-startup(2)'s SLOT_FD_BASE
    /// below the process's limit of files (radix-process(2)'s `limits`),
    /// as POSIX's lowest free descriptor, and writes their numbers into
    /// `slots` (u32 each). Returns how many bytes and how many files it
    /// put in slots, and how many files came that `slots` or the slots
    /// had no room for, which are closed. Needs the read right.
    method receive_files = 20 (buffer: out bytes, slots: out bytes, flags: SocketFlags)
        -> (count: u64, files: u32, lost: u32)
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, missing_rights, bad_address, no_memory, not_socket,
                not_connected, would_block, interrupted];
    /// Binds a network socket (radix-network(2)'s `socket`) to `address`,
    /// an Address: to one of the machine's addresses, or to any (all
    /// zero), and to a port, or with port 0 to one the kernel picks from
    /// EPHEMERAL_FIRST up. A port of its own needs the receive right on
    /// the network capability in slot `network`, and a port in its range,
    /// else missing_rights or out_of_range; port 0 needs only the
    /// capability, and a socket bound so can listen if the capability
    /// has the receive right for the port picked. A socket bound already
    /// fails with invalid_argument, and one of another family with
    /// not_supported.
    method bind_address = 21 (network: slot, address: in bytes) -> ()
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, wrong_kind, missing_rights, invalid_argument,
                bad_address, out_of_range, not_socket, not_supported, address_in_use,
                address_not_available, busy];
    /// Connects a network socket to `address`, an Address, through the
    /// network capability in slot `network`, which needs the send right,
    /// or for an address on this machine's networks the local right
    /// (radix-network(2)).
    /// A stream socket makes a connection over the interface with a route
    /// to it, waiting until it is made unless the description is
    /// non_blocking (in_progress; `poll` finds the socket writable once it
    /// is made or has failed); one that listens fails with
    /// invalid_argument. A datagram socket takes `address` as where its
    /// datagrams go and the only place they come from. A socket that is
    /// not bound is first bound to any address and a port the kernel
    /// picks.
    method connect_address = 22 (network: slot, address: in bytes) -> ()
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, wrong_kind, missing_rights, invalid_argument,
                bad_address, not_socket, not_supported, address_in_use, no_buffers,
                no_memory, unreachable, refused, reset, timed_out, in_progress, already,
                connected, interrupted, busy];
    /// Sends `data` as one datagram to `address`, an Address, from a
    /// datagram or ping socket, through the network capability in slot
    /// `network`, which needs the send right, or the local right for an
    /// address on this machine's networks, a broadcast or a multicast one
    /// (radix-network(2)); returns how many bytes went. A socket that is not bound is bound first, as by
    /// `connect_address`. A stream socket sends as `send` does, and
    /// takes neither the capability nor the address. Needs the write
    /// right.
    method send_to = 23 (network: slot, address: in bytes, data: in bytes, flags: SocketFlags)
        -> (count: u64)
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, wrong_kind, missing_rights, invalid_argument, bad_address,
                not_socket, not_supported, address_in_use, no_buffers, no_memory, unreachable,
                would_block, interrupted, message_too_long, not_connected, gone, reset, broadcast,
                refused, host_unreachable, protocol_error, host_down, no_network,
                protocol_unreachable, prohibited];
    /// Receives as `receive` does, and writes where what came is from
    /// into `address`, as an Address, if it has room for one; returns how
    /// many bytes came. Needs the read right.
    method receive_from = 24 (buffer: out bytes, address: out bytes, flags: SocketFlags)
        -> (count: u64)
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, missing_rights, bad_address, not_socket, not_connected,
                would_block, interrupted, reset, refused, unreachable, host_unreachable,
                protocol_error, host_down, no_network, protocol_unreachable, prohibited,
                message_too_long, not_supported];
    /// Reads, and with `set` changes to `value`, one of a network
    /// socket's options, and returns it as it was. A read-only option
    /// fails with invalid_argument when set, and a socket that is not a
    /// network one with not_supported.
    method socket_option = 25 (option: SocketOption, set: bool, value: u64) -> (value: u64)
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, invalid_argument, not_socket, not_supported];
    /// Changes the file's owner, group or permission bits, as
    /// radix-directory(2)'s `set_access` does, each that is its KEEP
    /// staying (ADR 0213), with the owners radix-directory(2)'s
    /// `set_access` needs. Pipes, sockets and devices keep none, and
    /// fail with not_permitted. Needs the write right.
    method set_access = 26 (owner: u32, group: u32, mode: u32) -> ()
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, missing_rights, invalid_argument, not_permitted,
                read_only, no_space, quota_exceeded, io, corrupt];
    /// Copies the value of the file's extended attribute `name` to
    /// `buffer`, as radix-directory(2)'s `get_attribute` does, and
    /// returns its length. Pipes and sockets keep none (not_supported).
    /// Needs no right, as fgetxattr(2) needs none.
    method get_attribute = 27 (name: in bytes, buffer: out bytes) -> (len: u64)
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, invalid_argument, no_memory, bad_address, denied,
                no_attribute, out_of_range, not_supported, io, corrupt];
    /// Sets the file's extended attribute `name` to `value`, as
    /// radix-directory(2)'s `set_attribute` does with its `flags` but
    /// no_follow. Needs the write right.
    method set_attribute = 28 (name: in bytes, value: in bytes, flags: AttributeFlags)
        -> ()
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, missing_rights, invalid_argument, no_memory,
                bad_address, denied, not_permitted, exists, no_attribute, out_of_range,
                arguments_too_long, not_supported, read_only, no_space, quota_exceeded, io,
                corrupt];
    /// Copies the names of the file's extended attributes to `buffer`, as
    /// radix-directory(2)'s `list_attributes` does, and returns their
    /// length. Needs no right.
    method list_attributes = 29 (buffer: out bytes) -> (len: u64)
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, invalid_argument, no_memory, bad_address, out_of_range,
                not_supported, io, corrupt];
    /// Removes the file's extended attribute `name`, as
    /// radix-directory(2)'s `remove_attribute` does. Needs the write
    /// right.
    method remove_attribute = 30 (name: in bytes) -> ()
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, missing_rights, invalid_argument, no_memory,
                bad_address, denied, not_permitted, no_attribute, out_of_range, not_supported,
                read_only, no_space, io, corrupt];
    /// With `join`, has a datagram socket take the datagrams sent to the
    /// multicast group `group`, an Address whose port is not looked at,
    /// on the interface numbered `interface`: 0 for the one the routes
    /// to the group go through. Joining needs the local right on the
    /// network capability in slot `network` (radix-network(2)); a
    /// socket joins 20 groups at most (no_buffers), each once
    /// (address_in_use), and only a multicast address (invalid_argument)
    /// on an interface that is there, or with 0 when a route goes to the
    /// group (no_device, as on Linux). Without
    /// `join`, leaves the group, which the socket must have joined there
    /// (address_not_available), whatever `network` holds. A datagram to
    /// a group comes only to the sockets on its port that joined it on
    /// the interface it came by (ADR 0336). Closing the socket leaves
    /// its groups. (Since version 10.)
    method socket_group = 31 (network: slot, join: bool, group: in bytes, interface: u32) -> ()
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, wrong_kind, missing_rights, invalid_argument,
                bad_address, not_socket, not_supported, address_in_use, address_not_available,
                no_buffers, no_device];
    /// Receives as `receive_from` does, and writes where the datagram
    /// went into `destination`, an Address, if it has room for one: the
    /// address of this machine's or the group it was sent to, and the
    /// port. Returns how many bytes came, the number of the interface
    /// it came by (0 for a stream socket's), and whether the socket's
    /// packet_info option is set. (Since version 10.)
    method receive_message = 32 (buffer: out bytes, address: out bytes, destination: out bytes, flags: SocketFlags)
        -> (count: u64, interface: u32, info: bool)
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, missing_rights, bad_address, not_socket, not_connected,
                would_block, interrupted, reset, refused, unreachable, host_unreachable,
                protocol_error, host_down, no_network, protocol_unreachable, prohibited,
                message_too_long, not_supported];
    /// Takes the oldest of the ICMP and ICMPv6 errors a datagram or ping
    /// socket heard about what it sent, which wait while its
    /// receive_errors option is set: POSIX's MSG_ERRQUEUE, as on Linux
    /// (ADR 0345). Writes what the error quotes of the packet into
    /// `buffer`, past a datagram's UDP header or from an echo request's
    /// own header on, the first 128 bytes at most, what `buffer` has no
    /// room for lost; and what it says into `report`, a SocketError
    /// (radix-network(2)), if it has room for one. Returns how many bytes
    /// it wrote, and how many it kept. Fails with would_block, waiting
    /// for none, when none waits, and with not_supported for a socket of
    /// another kind. The send or receive after it fails with the next
    /// error's, if another waits. Needs the read right. (Since version
    /// 11.)
    method receive_error = 33 (buffer: out bytes, report: out bytes) -> (count: u64, kept: u64)
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, missing_rights, bad_address, not_socket,
                not_supported, would_block];
}
